ting Go loading function...\n\nNow let me trace through the test cases to verify everything holds up, starting with the ordering test.\n\nWith map `{\"b\", \"a\"}` the in-degrees are both zero, so the queue sorts alphabetically giving `[\"a\", \"b\"]` consistently across repeated runs. For the self-cycle case, node x depends on itself, giving it an in-degree of 1 with an empty queue, so the result list stays empty and fails the length check against the graph size, correctly triggering a cycle error. Moving to the plan test, I'm tracing through a dependency chain of app→lib→base to confirm the build order resolves properly.\n\nTracing through the topological sort: base has zero dependencies so it's processed first, which drops lib's in-degree to zero, then lib's processing drops app's in-degree to zero, yielding the expected order \"base,lib,app\". I then verify the missing-dependency test correctly throws an error, and set up the two-node cycle case (x→y→x) to confirm cycle detection catches it.\n\nMoving to parser validation, duplicate names and empty names both correctly produce errors. For the cache test, I trace through four scenarios: two distinct cache misses that compute and store results, one failed build that errors without caching, and a final build that hits the cache from the first successful call. Now I'm starting to trace through the batch test scenario.\n\nChecking the cycle case confirms Plan.Build returns an error and main exits with status 1, as expected. With the test cases verified, I'm ready to write the actual implementation, starting with graph.go using the sort and fmt packages.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_read_input_tokens":18291,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":515},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context